---
term: Charter
section: building-blocks
order: 20
definition: The founding document that creates a corporation and defines its purpose, membership, jurisdiction, objectives, governance, and relationships.
seeAlso:
  - chartered-corporation
  - charter-formation
  - executable-constitutionalism
---
The charter is the fundamental institutional primitive of the Social Charter. Every institution, from a single person to the highest human authority, is an instance of the same chartered form. A charter is an operational specification, not a ceremonial document. At a minimum, a charter defines the following:

- Purpose and [[common interest]]
- Membership
- [[Jurisdiction]]
- [[Asset|Assets]] and [[commons]]
- [[Objective function]]
- Rights and obligations
- [[Delegation]] powers
- Executive authority
- [[Arbitration]] and conflict-resolution mechanisms
- [[Measurement mechanism|Measurement]] and [[audit]] mechanisms
- [[Amendment]] and evolution procedures
- [[Dissolution]] and [[exit]] procedures
- Relationships with parent, constituent, and sibling corporations
